Numerous clinical studies have demonstrated that regular physical activity can significantly reduce depressive symptoms, often matching the efficacy of antidepressant medications.
Exercise triggers the release of endorphins, which are known to create a sense of euphoria and improve mood, similar to the effects of certain medications.
Increased serotonin levels from exercise contribute to improved mood regulation, as serotonin is a key neurotransmitter involved in emotional well-being.
The physiological changes induced by exercise, such as improved sleep and reduced inflammation, also play a role in alleviating depressive symptoms.
Long-term engagement in exercise has been linked to sustained improvements in mental health, suggesting a lasting impact on brain chemistry.